Technological determinism is a term that actually encompasses a wide range of ways of thinking, starting with technological propulsion and technical norms, to the exact meaning that human destiny is driven by the underlying logic associated with scientific laws and their manifestation in technology.
Most interpretations of technological determinism share two general theories: one is that the development of technology itself follows a path that goes far beyond the cultural and political influence, and the other is that technology is not socially conditioned, but rather has an “impact” on the underlying society.
For example, it may be said that cars inevitably lead to the development of roads.
However, only a handful of cars do not make the national road network profitable, so a new production method has been developed to lower the cost of car ownership.
The increase in the number of car owners will lead to an increase in the incidence of road accidents, resulting in the development of new technologies in the medical field to treat injured bodies.
